// run
// #5521: a NAMED (non-arrow) function's own `return <expr>?`, where `<expr>`
// unwraps to a VOID ok type, panicked the compiler with a bare
// `panic: assertion failed`, rc=2, no file, no line.
//
// `emitReturnValue` (compiler/lowerfunc.bit) — the ONE place every return
// form builds its `Ret` (`return a, b`, `return t`, `return f()`) — put the
// void expression's own instruction id into `Op.Ret.args` regardless of the
// declared result type. A void value has no register class; when it was
// defined in an EARLIER block than the `ret` — what `other()?` produces, the
// value arriving in the branch's ok successor — emission resolved it to x19
// against a frame that had saved nothing, and #3101's assertSavedCoversWrites
// (compiler/arm64ctx.bit) fired. Identical invariant, identical assertion
// offset, to #4571's panic for an expression-bodied ARROW (`closureExprReturn`,
// compiler/lowerfuncval.bit) — but this is the statement-form `return <expr>`
// path, a different function, that never got the equivalent guard.
//
// Row 1 (`writeNode`) is the panicking shape. Row 2 (`writeNodeStmt`) is the
// discriminating control that already worked and must keep working: the same
// void-fallible call as a STATEMENT followed by a bare `return`, instead of
// `return <call>?`. Row 3 (`writeNodeInt`) is the other control: the
// identical `return <call>?` shape with a NON-void ok type, which must still
// return its value through the register.

fn other(): ()! {
  return
}

fn writeNode(): ()! {
  return other()?
}

fn writeNodeStmt(): ()! {
  other()?
  return
}

fn otherInt(): i64! {
  return 7
}

fn writeNodeInt(): i64! {
  return otherInt()?
}

fn main(): ()! {
  writeNode()?
  println("void: ok")
  writeNodeStmt()?
  println("stmt: ok")
  println("int: ${writeNodeInt()?}")
  return
}
